+### 5.4 Why Appendix II can still kill the controls
+
+**SOURCE-READ.** Pages 208--209 do not derive an adjacent-\(p,q\) coefficient
+map for the reduced \((16,12)\) case. Moh takes the major chart
+\(\sigma=\pi t^{1/4}\), a minor chart
+\[
+ \sigma^*=t^{-1}+a_0+a_1t+a_2t^2+\pi t^3,
+\]
+and a compatible truncation ending in \(\pi t^{11/4}\). He makes these a
+coherent system for the *same* transformed \(\bar g,\bar f\), introduces a
+common approximate root \(h\), and writes
+\[
+ \bar g=h^4+\alpha_1h^3+\alpha_2h^2+\alpha_3h+\alpha_4,\qquad
+ \bar f=h^3+\beta_2h+\beta_3.
+\]
+The subsequent equations use a shared polynomial shape for \(h\) and its
+coefficients.
+
+Pages 210--211 likewise use, for \((15,10)\),
+\[
+ f=h^2+2\beta,\qquad
+ \beta^2=\alpha h+\gamma,\qquad
+ \beta^3=(3\gamma+a)h^2+\varepsilon h+\delta,
+\]
+plus the shared polynomial forms of \(h,\beta\) forced by the three root
+subdiscs. The degree equations split on a common coefficient and contradict
+polynomiality.
+
+Those are deeper common-\((f,g,h,\beta)\) coefficient computations. The SAT
+results in §§5.1--5.2 are therefore the expected positive controls for the
+coarser two-node projection, not disagreements with Appendix II.
+
